From: Vahiru <nekovahiru@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2_codec2: set the minimum resolution on the compressed
 input queue

setupInputFormat() sets the format of the OUTPUT (compressed) queue with
ui::Size(), which is -1 x -1, not 0 x 0. buildV4L2Format() stores it in
the __u32 width and height, and venus clamps 0xFFFFFFFF to 8192x8192.
It computes the hardware load from that size at streamon, before the
first SOURCE_CHANGE event, and fails every stream with "HW can't
support this load". ChromeOS drivers ignore the resolution of the
compressed queue.

Set the minimum resolution the device reports for the input format, as
setupMinimalOutputFormat() does for the CAPTURE queue, or 128x128 when
the device reports none. The real resolution arrives with the source
change event.

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp b/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
index 52b63b1..1a5fbf8 100644
--- a/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
+++ b/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
@@ -202,7 +202,13 @@ bool V4L2Decoder::setupInputFormat(const uint32_t inputPixelFormat, const size_t
     }
 
     // Setup the input format.
-    auto format = mInputQueue->setFormat(inputPixelFormat, ui::Size(), inputBufferSize, 0);
+    // Drivers such as venus compute the load from the resolution set on the compressed
+    // queue. ui::Size() is -1 x -1 and would be clamped to the maximum, so use the
+    // minimum resolution the device reports.
+    ui::Size inputMinRes, inputMaxRes;
+    mDevice->getSupportedResolution(inputPixelFormat, &inputMinRes, &inputMaxRes);
+    if (inputMinRes.isEmpty()) inputMinRes.set(128, 128);
+    auto format = mInputQueue->setFormat(inputPixelFormat, inputMinRes, inputBufferSize, 0);
     if (!format) {
         ALOGE("Failed to call IOCTL to set input format.");
         return false;
